[{"_1":2},"llms/mdx",{"_3":4},"data","# Renewal and recovery (/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/renewal-and-recovery)\n\n\n\nRenewal retains the device identifier and requests a successor certificate. The client must complete the process: check the response, install the matching pair and demonstrate that the consumer accepts it. Lamassu alerts notify you; they do not perform those steps.\n\nPrepare renewal [#prepare-renewal]\n\n* Query the current certificate in Lamassu and compare it with the installed certificate. You need its subject, serial and dates, as well as the client key.\n* Check the **re-enrollment** authentication mode: it is independent of initial authentication. With `CLIENT_CERTIFICATE`, use the current identity; do not assume the bootstrap credential is admitted.\n* Calculate the window's start: the current certificate's `NotAfter` minus `reenrollment_delta`. Preventive and critical thresholds produce states/notifications; they do not extend that window.\n* Review **Revoke on Re-enrollment** and recovery if installation fails. When enabled, Lamassu can revoke the previous identity before the client installs the new one.\n\nRequest the successor certificate [#request-the-successor-certificate]\n\nThe example continues the [EST tutorial](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/quickstarts/enroll-device), in the same Bash session with `set -euo pipefail`, `umask 077` and `EST_BASE`. Use a new key compatible with the profile. Conversion from the certificate preserves the subject and copies only SAN when present; check the subject and requested names before sending it. The backend checks the subject against the current identity: it attempts RawSubject equality and then compares CN, OU, O, L, ST and C. Rebuilding only the CN can lose required attributes.\n\n```bash\ntest ! -e device.next.key\ntest ! -e device.next.crt\nopenssl genpkey -algorithm EC -pkeyopt ec_paramgen_curve:P-256 -out device.next.key\nopenssl x509 -x509toreq -in device.crt -key device.next.key -sha256 \\\n  -copy_extensions copy -ext subjectAltName -out device-renewal.csr\nopenssl req -in device-renewal.csr -verify -noout -subject\nopenssl req -in device-renewal.csr -outform DER -out device-renewal.csr.der\nopenssl base64 -A -in device-renewal.csr.der -out device-renewal.csr.b64\ncurl --fail-with-body --show-error --silent --max-time 30 \\\n  --cacert est-trust.pem --cert device.crt --key device.key \\\n  --header 'Content-Type: application/pkcs10' \\\n  --data-binary @device-renewal.csr.b64 \\\n  --dump-header renewal.headers --output renewal.b64 \\\n  \"$EST_BASE/simplereenroll\"\nopenssl base64 -d -in renewal.b64 -out renewal.p7b\nopenssl pkcs7 -inform DER -in renewal.p7b -print_certs -quiet -out device.next.crt\nopenssl verify -CAfile device-root.pem -purpose sslclient device.next.crt\nopenssl x509 -in device.next.crt -noout -subject -issuer -serial -dates\nopenssl x509 -in device.next.crt -pubkey -noout > next-certificate-public.pem\nopenssl pkey -pubin -in next-certificate-public.pem -outform DER -out next-certificate-public.der\nopenssl pkey -in device.next.key -pubout -outform DER -out next-key-public.der\ncmp next-certificate-public.der next-key-public.der\n```\n\nThe request authenticates with `device.crt` and `device.key`; the CSR contains the new public key. Also check extensions, dates and correspondence of the serial with the current slot in Lamassu. Do not resubmit a failed request without querying that state: issuance, previous revocation and successor binding can have partial results.\n\nInstall and check the new pair [#install-and-check-the-new-pair]\n\nKeep the result as `device.next.crt` and `device.next.key` until verified. Switch the pair through a coordinated operation in the device store or configuration; avoid temporarily exposing a certificate with another version's key.\n\nTest mTLS with the consumer and verify that it uses the new serial. Confirm the history and current certificate in Lamassu. If **Revoke on Re-enrollment** was disabled, the previous identity can remain valid: retire it according to PKI policy after testing the successor, and check revocation publication and consumption.\n\nIf the previous identity is already revoked, retaining its files does not restore a valid connection. Recovery requires an admitted credential and authorized intervention according to the situation.\n\nRecover according to state [#recover-according-to-state]\n\n| Situation                                 | Procedure                                                                                                                                                                          |\n| ----------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Valid certificate, outside the window     | Schedule the request within the window. Do not use `simpleenroll` to bypass it.                                                                                                    |\n| Expired certificate, key available        | With certificate authentication, review `enable_expired_renewal` and whether Gateway permits presenting that credential. Revocation, subject and the remaining checks still apply. |\n| Renewal through webhook or `NO_AUTH`      | The mTLS expiry check does not run in these modes. Define expiry and authorization policy in the corresponding admission process; the flag is not a universal prohibition.         |\n| Lost or inaccessible key                  | You cannot prove possession of the previous identity. Authorize new admission and, if appropriate, controlled replacement; revoke the compromised identity.                        |\n| Current identity revoked                  | `simplereenroll` rejects the revoked current certificate. Investigate the reason and prepare an authorized readmission procedure; do not disable authentication to force it.       |\n| Device without an identity                | Use initial enrollment. For an existing entry, check the replacement restriction in [provisioning](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/provisioning-strategies).                  |\n| Retired device                            | Review the operational decision before readmission. Decommissioned state and revocation of the entire history are not equivalent.                                                  |\n| Timeout, HTTP 500 or installation failure | Inspect the device, issued certificates and history before retrying. A successor may have been issued or the previous identity revoked.                                            |\n\nContinuous operation [#continuous-operation]\n\nSchedule renewal with time for disconnections, trust distribution and consumer checks. Stagger requests across the population and bound retries within the window; authentication, subject and policy errors require correcting their cause. Maintain an admitted and tested recovery mechanism for lost keys and pairs that could not be installed.\n\nContinue with [Trust distribution](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/trust-distribution), [Devices and identities](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/device-management) and [Troubleshooting](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/troubleshooting).\n"]
